**Read:** [Data Evaluation: Theory and Practice for Materials Properties — Ronald G. Munro](../sources/Data-Evaluation-for-Materials-Properties-NIST.lingua#r_7N31EWH1JVE54EM3BRSQGYS04K), evaluation and uncertainty; [Mesoscale tensile blind predictions — AM-Bench 2022](../sources/Mesoscale-Tensile-Blind-Predictions-AM-Bench.lingua#r_72TG9WD15YP5DCTVCCX26W7FND), measurement uncertainty; [Verifying precision in density-functional calculations — Bosoni and colleagues](../sources/Precision-of-DFT-Implementations-Bosoni.lingua#r_4N8GPD0R7RGEZSFDT5SYHCNQF5), numerical convergence and protocol precision; shared metrology foundations.

Observation noise, sampling variation, uncertain parameters, numerical approximation and missing physics are different sources of disagreement. They can interact and need not be independent or normally distributed. Propagating a parameter covariance conditional on one model does not quantify every alternative model.

For a differentiable response y=f(θ), a local approximation gives variance JΣJᵀ, retaining parameter covariance. Check its validity against nonlinearity, boundaries and alternative scenarios. A prediction interval whose nominal coverage fails on held-out cases needs revision. Common calibration errors can correlate many observations and resist averaging away.

**Work:** Build an uncertainty account for one earlier investigation. Compare local propagation with direct sampling or bounded scenarios; verify dimensions and correlations. Separate numerical refinement from physical-parameter variation. Construct a case where narrow conditional intervals miss observations because the model omits a mechanism, and propose an experiment that distinguishes that explanation from a calibration error.

